{"parse":{"title":"Liz and the Blue Bird","pageid":60304198,"revid":1383036815,"text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading3\"><h3 id=\"Background\">Background</h3></div>\n<figure class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File/Thumb\"><a href=\"/wiki/File:Kyoto_Animation_Studio_1a.jpg\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Kyoto_Animation_Studio_1a.jpg/250px-Kyoto_Animation_Studio_1a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"250\" height=\"209\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Kyoto_Animation_Studio_1a.jpg/500px-Kyoto_Animation_Studio_1a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"3476\" data-file-height=\"2905\" /></a><figcaption><i>Liz and the Blue Bird</i> was produced by Kyoto Animation (studio building pictured 2015).</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Beginning in 2015, the <i><a href=\"/wiki/Sound!_Euphonium\" title=\"Sound! Euphonium\">Sound! Euphonium</a></i> novels by Ayano Takeda were adapted into an animated television series by <a href=\"/wiki/Kyoto_Animation\" title=\"Kyoto Animation\">Kyoto Animation</a>.<sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-1\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-1\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>1<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup> After being offered a film adaptation of her series by Kyoto Animation, Takeda submitted the plot of what would become <i>The Turbulent Second Movement</i><span style=\"font-weight: normal\"> (<span title=\"Japanese-language text\"><span lang=\"ja\">波乱の第二楽章</span></span>)</span> installments of her novel series to the studio. The plot featured the main character of the series, Kumiko Oumae, as well as the side characters of Nozomi and Mizore.<sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-ExciteInterview1&#95;2-0\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-ExciteInterview1-2\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>2<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup> <a href=\"/wiki/Tatsuya_Ishihara\" title=\"Tatsuya Ishihara\">Tatsuya Ishihara</a>, the director of the television adaptation, originally planned to adapt Takeda's plot as a single film. However, <a href=\"/wiki/Naoko_Yamada\" title=\"Naoko Yamada\">Naoko Yamada</a>, who worked with Ishihara as the show's series director, suggested that Nozomi and Mizore's story could stand on its own as a <a href=\"/wiki/Spin-off_(media)\" title=\"Spin-off (media)\">spin-off</a> film. <i>Liz and the Blue Bird</i> emerged when Kyoto Animation decided to split the adaptation between two films; Ishihara directed the film about Kumiko, while Yamada directed the film about Nozomi and Mizore.<sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-animeanime&#95;3-0\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-animeanime-3\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>3<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup><sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-animedia&#95;4-0\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-animedia-4\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>4<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup> Ishihara's film, <i><a href=\"/wiki/Sound!_Euphonium:_The_Movie_%E2%80%93_Our_Promise:_A_Brand_New_Day\" title=\"Sound! Euphonium: The Movie – Our Promise: A Brand New Day\">Sound! Euphonium: The Movie – Our Promise: A Brand New Day</a></i>, was released in 2019.<sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-5\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-5\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>5<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup> \n</p><p>With the production staff from <i>Sound! Euphonium</i> occupied with Ishihara's film, several people who had worked under Yamada's direction for the 2016 film <a href=\"/wiki/A_Silent_Voice_(film)\" title=\"A Silent Voice (film)\"><i>A Silent Voice</i></a> returned to produce <i>Liz and the Blue Bird</i>.<sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-zing&#95;6-0\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-zing-6\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>6<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup> These included writer <a href=\"/wiki/Reiko_Yoshida\" title=\"Reiko Yoshida\">Reiko Yoshida</a>, composer <a href=\"/wiki/Kensuke_Ushio\" title=\"Kensuke Ushio\">Kensuke Ushio</a>, and character designer <a href=\"/wiki/Futoshi_Nishiya\" title=\"Futoshi Nishiya\">Futoshi Nishiya</a>.<sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-7\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-7\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>7<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup> Yoshida also worked as the screenwriter for several other works directed by Yamada, including <a href=\"/wiki/K-On!_(TV_series)\" title=\"K-On! (TV series)\"><i>K-On!</i></a> and <i><a href=\"/wiki/Tamako_Market\" title=\"Tamako Market\">Tamako Market</a></i>.<sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-8\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-8\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>8<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup> Character designer Futoshi Nishiya re-designed the characters instead of using the designs from <i>Sound Euphonium!</i>, in order to better fit the film's style, story and themes.<sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-MangaTokyoInterview&#95;9-0\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-MangaTokyoInterview-9\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>9<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup><sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-OfficialYamadaInterview&#95;10-0\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-OfficialYamadaInterview-10\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>10<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup><sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-ExciteInterview1&#95;2-1\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-ExciteInterview1-2\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>2<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup><sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-HollywoodReporterReview&#95;11-0\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-HollywoodReporterReview-11\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>11<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup> The crew also included Mutsuo Shinohara as <a href=\"/wiki/Art_director\" title=\"Art director\">art director</a>, and Naomi Ishida as color stylist. <a href=\"/wiki/Stephanie_Sheh\" title=\"Stephanie Sheh\">Stephanie Sheh</a> served as <a href=\"/wiki/Casting_director\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Casting director\">casting director</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/Dubbing_(filmmaking)\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Dubbing (filmmaking)\">ADR director</a> for the film's English release.<sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-HollywoodReporterReview&#95;11-1\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-HollywoodReporterReview-11\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>11<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup><sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-ANNHomeRelease&#95;12-0\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-ANNHomeRelease-12\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>12<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup>\n</p>\n<div class=\"mw-references-wrap mw-references-columns\"><ol class=\"references\">\n<li id=\"cite&#95;note-1\"><span class=\"mw-cite-backlink\"><b><a href=\"#cite_ref-1\">^</a></b></span> <span class=\"reference-text\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r1333433106\">.mw-parser-output cite.citation{font-style:inherit;word-wrap:break-word}.mw-parser-output .citation q{quotes:\"\\\"\"\"\\\"\"\"'\"\"'\"}.mw-parser-output .citation:target{background-color:rgba(0,127,255,0.133)}.mw-parser-output .id-lock-free.id-lock-free a{background:url(\"//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Lock-green.svg\")right 0.1em center/9px no-repeat}.mw-parser-output .id-lock-limited.id-lock-limited a,.mw-parser-output .id-lock-registration.id-lock-registration a{background:url(\"//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d6/Lock-gray-alt-2.svg\")right 0.1em center/9px no-repeat}.mw-parser-output .id-lock-subscription.id-lock-subscription a{background:url(\"//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/Lock-